[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186162041 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-79df1f11-1c43-51b8-8e99-4f8efb003972"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-79df1f11-1c43-51b8-8e99-4f8efb003972" :question/id #uuid "79df1f11-1c43-51b8-8e99-4f8efb003972" :question/math-academy-id "q-140961" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-6I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-1I)v=0$ gives the unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V:$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c4360e51-0f64-590c-bba8-917045fffada"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c4360e51-0f64-590c-bba8-917045fffada" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4360e51-0f64-590c-bba8-917045fffada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c0ca0393-d174-5ef6-b704-b99b7d145991" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ea8b7cd-7674-5bdd-be23-c96008f18ef2" "answer-a39bb8ab-4347-5457-ac3e-775c486b4c6f" "answer-1a42c0f3-23fe-54b3-9812-4384378e4fdf" "answer-c0ca0393-d174-5ef6-b704-b99b7d145991" "answer-f50be767-9717-528b-bc60-edef8d33bf76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ea8b7cd-7674-5bdd-be23-c96008f18ef2" :answer/id #uuid "2ea8b7cd-7674-5bdd-be23-c96008f18ef2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a39bb8ab-4347-5457-ac3e-775c486b4c6f" :answer/id #uuid "a39bb8ab-4347-5457-ac3e-775c486b4c6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\